1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:03,800
CareSlot: a clinic appointment scheduler, shipped from a laptop to a monitored Kubernetes cluster.

2
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:07,000
Clinics still book on paper and spreadsheets, so doctors get double-booked.

3
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:09,800
CareSlot's core rule: a doctor can never be double-booked.

4
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:13,000
React front desk, FastAPI backend, PostgreSQL.

5
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:15,800
Overlapping bookings are rejected with a 409 conflict.

6
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:19,500
The stack, layer by layer: application, quality, containers, CI/CD,

7
00:00:19,500 --> 00:00:22,800
security, infrastructure, Kubernetes and observability.

8
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:26,500
Push → GitHub Actions tests, scans and builds → images in GHCR tagged with the commit SHA

9
00:00:26,500 --> 00:00:29,800
→ CI writes the SHA into Git → Argo CD syncs the cluster → Prometheus and Grafana watch it.

10
00:00:30,200 --> 00:00:33,500
Four quality gates in parallel, then build, Trivy gate and push to GHCR.

11
00:00:33,500 --> 00:00:36,800
A throwaway kind cluster books a real appointment before anything is promoted.

12
00:00:37,200 --> 00:00:40,000
Trivy blocked the first backend image: 7 HIGH CVEs, including CVE-2025-62727 in Starlette.

13
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:42,800
Fixed by upgrading and stripping pip. Both images now scan clean.

14
00:00:43,200 --> 00:00:46,000
Terraform modules: a VPC with public and private subnets across two AZs,

15
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:48,800
and EKS with a managed node group. Plan, apply, destroy: 28 resources.

16
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:52,000
Helm chart: 2 replicas each, ClusterIP services, an Ingress for / and /api,

17
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:54,800
non-root pods and Pod Security. Argo CD does the deploying.

18
00:00:55,200 --> 00:01:00,800
Under load, the HPA scaled the backend from 2 to 6 pods, then back down.

19
00:01:01,200 --> 00:01:04,000
Prometheus scrapes /metrics through a ServiceMonitor.

20
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:06,800
Grafana shows traffic, latency, errors, CPU per pod and booking metrics.

21
00:01:07,200 --> 00:01:10,500
Learnings: scanners find real problems, and start-up order is a deployment concern.

22
00:01:10,500 --> 00:01:13,800
GitOps keeps the cluster boring, and troubleshooting is the job.

23
00:01:14,200 --> 00:01:16,800
Now the live walkthrough.

24
00:01:17,400 --> 00:01:27,500
The repository: backend, frontend, Helm chart, Terraform, Argo CD, monitoring and CI, each in its own folder.

25
00:01:27,500 --> 00:01:32,500
appointments.py: ALLOWED_TRANSITIONS defines the appointment status workflow.

26
00:01:32,500 --> 00:01:37,500
_ensure_slot_free is the double-booking rule: any overlap for the same doctor raises a 409.

27
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:42,500
conftest.py swaps the database for in-memory SQLite, so tests never touch Postgres.

28
00:01:42,500 --> 00:01:46,500
Multi-stage Dockerfile. The runtime image runs as non-root user 10001.

29
00:01:46,500 --> 00:01:50,500
Helm templates: deployments, services, Ingress, HPA, ServiceMonitor and Grafana dashboard.

30
00:01:50,500 --> 00:01:55,500
Terraform EKS module: IAM roles, KMS key, the cluster and its managed node group.

31
00:01:55,500 --> 00:01:59,500
Argo CD application: tracks the chart on main with auto-sync, prune and self-heal.

32
00:01:59,500 --> 00:02:05,300
The CI/CD workflow that runs everything shown next.

33
00:02:06,033 --> 00:02:08,702
Here is the app, served through the ingress.

34
00:02:08,702 --> 00:02:13,189
I try to book Ananya with an orthopedist at a time the doctor already has.

35
00:02:13,189 --> 00:02:16,767
The API rejects it with a conflict, and the form shows why.

36
00:02:16,767 --> 00:02:19,678
I pick a free time and the booking goes through.

37
00:02:19,678 --> 00:02:22,771
Then I check the patient in and complete the visit.

38
00:02:22,771 --> 00:02:26,773
The patients tab has search, the doctors tab shows who is on duty,

39
00:02:26,773 --> 00:02:30,958
and FastAPI generates interactive API docs for all sixteen endpoints.

40
00:02:30,958 --> 00:02:32,474
Notice the sidebar label.

41
00:02:32,474 --> 00:02:34,172
We'll change it in a moment.

42
00:02:35,067 --> 00:02:36,638
Now CI and CD, end to end.

43
00:02:36,638 --> 00:02:40,024
I make a visible change: a release label in the sidebar.

44
00:02:40,024 --> 00:02:44,256
First the recent history, then a one-line change to the sidebar label,

45
00:02:44,256 --> 00:02:45,767
and the diff to prove it.

46
00:02:45,767 --> 00:02:47,520
I commit it and push to main.

47
00:02:47,520 --> 00:02:49,999
That push is the only thing I do by hand.

48
00:02:49,999 --> 00:02:52,115
Everything after this is automated.

49
00:02:54,600 --> 00:02:57,987
The push triggers the CareSlot pipeline in GitHub Actions.

50
00:02:57,987 --> 00:03:01,198
The four quality gates are already running in parallel.

51
00:03:01,198 --> 00:03:06,570
The backend job lints with Ruff and runs pytest, the frontend job runs the production build,

52
00:03:06,570 --> 00:03:10,132
the infrastructure job runs terraform validate and helm lint,

53
00:03:10,132 --> 00:03:14,044
and the security job scans the whole repository for leaked secrets.

54
00:03:14,044 --> 00:03:18,132
The image jobs for the backend and frontend wait for all four to pass.

55
00:03:18,132 --> 00:03:20,934
If any gate fails, nothing gets built or pushed.

56
00:03:20,934 --> 00:03:24,496
After the images, the deploy and GitOps jobs run in sequence.

57
00:03:25,400 --> 00:03:29,506
From the terminal, every job finished green: security, frontend,

58
00:03:29,506 --> 00:03:35,088
Terraform and Helm validation, backend tests, both image builds with their Trivy gates,

59
00:03:35,088 --> 00:03:38,680
the deploy smoke test on kind, and the GitOps promotion.

60
00:03:38,680 --> 00:03:40,541
About three minutes in total.

61
00:03:41,433 --> 00:03:44,010
In the browser, the run graph is all green.

62
00:03:44,010 --> 00:03:48,324
The registry has the new backend image, tagged with the full commit SHA,

63
00:03:48,324 --> 00:03:49,941
and there is no latest tag.

64
00:03:49,941 --> 00:03:54,974
In the commit history, the CI bot has added a GitOps commit, and in the values file,

65
00:03:54,974 --> 00:03:57,371
the image tags now point at the new SHA.

66
00:03:57,371 --> 00:03:59,528
That commit is what Argo CD deploys.

67
00:03:59,528 --> 00:04:04,621
Notice it's marked [skip ci], so the bot's commit doesn't trigger the pipeline again.

68
00:04:04,621 --> 00:04:09,834
The whole path, from my push to a promotable release, took a little over three minutes,

69
00:04:09,834 --> 00:04:13,009
with no manual steps and no credentials on my laptop.

70
00:04:24,867 --> 00:04:26,726
Locally, the same checks pass.

71
00:04:26,726 --> 00:04:31,373
Ruff is clean, and twenty-one tests pass with ninety-four percent coverage.

72
00:04:31,373 --> 00:04:36,641
Here is Trivy against the original backend image: exit code one, seven high findings,

73
00:04:36,641 --> 00:04:39,244
including the Starlette denial of service.

74
00:04:39,244 --> 00:04:43,334
And against the fixed image: exit code zero, nothing fixable left.

75
00:04:43,334 --> 00:04:43,705
In CI,

76
00:04:43,705 --> 00:04:49,159
this exact check is the gate that decides whether an image is allowed into the registry.

77
00:04:51,333 --> 00:04:53,052
Next, Terraform.

78
00:04:53,052 --> 00:04:58,960
The root module wires together the VPC and EKS modules.

79
00:04:58,960 --> 00:05:06,909
terraform plan shows twenty-eight resources to add: the VPC, four subnets,

80
00:05:06,909 --> 00:05:14,214
the internet and NAT gateways, route tables, IAM roles, the KMS key,

81
00:05:14,214 --> 00:05:17,974
the EKS cluster and its node group.

82
00:05:17,974 --> 00:05:21,304
Apply creates all twenty-eight.

83
00:05:21,304 --> 00:05:29,038
Through the AWS CLI, the EKS cluster reports active, on Kubernetes 1.33.

84
00:05:29,038 --> 00:05:33,765
Then destroy removes all twenty-eight again.

85
00:05:33,765 --> 00:05:42,036
The tfvars example file documents the region, cluster version and node sizes,

86
00:05:42,036 --> 00:05:49,233
and the defaults are sized for a small clinic: two t3 medium nodes.

87
00:05:50,033 --> 00:05:56,216
Here the AWS CLI reads back the VPC, its subnets across two availability zones,

88
00:05:56,216 --> 00:06:00,833
the EKS cluster and the node group, in the expected region.

89
00:06:01,633 --> 00:06:02,396
Now Argo CD.

90
00:06:02,396 --> 00:06:05,385
The CareSlot application is synced and healthy.

91
00:06:05,385 --> 00:06:11,235
Its latest sync is the GitOps commit that CI made a few minutes ago, deployed automatically,

92
00:06:11,235 --> 00:06:13,016
with nobody running kubectl.

93
00:06:13,016 --> 00:06:15,559
The history shows each previous release.

94
00:06:15,559 --> 00:06:20,074
To show self-healing, I scale the frontend down to one replica by hand.

95
00:06:20,074 --> 00:06:25,479
Argo CD detects the drift, the app goes to progressing, and it restores two replicas,

96
00:06:25,479 --> 00:06:26,497
back to healthy.

97
00:06:26,497 --> 00:06:32,284
The tree view shows every resource Argo CD manages: the deployments, replica sets and pods,

98
00:06:32,284 --> 00:06:36,099
the services, the ingress, the stateful set, the autoscaler,

99
00:06:36,099 --> 00:06:39,024
the service monitor and the Grafana dashboard.

100
00:06:40,200 --> 00:06:42,149
The same thing from the terminal.

101
00:06:42,149 --> 00:06:44,393
The application is synced and healthy.

102
00:06:44,393 --> 00:06:46,047
I scale the frontend to one.

103
00:06:46,047 --> 00:06:48,941
For a few seconds, only one of two pods is ready.

104
00:06:48,941 --> 00:06:52,130
Within about ten seconds, it's back to two out of two,

105
00:06:52,130 --> 00:06:54,965
and the application is still synced and healthy.

106
00:06:54,965 --> 00:06:58,567
Git is the source of truth, and manual drift doesn't survive.

107
00:07:03,533 --> 00:07:05,575
Here is the cluster with kubectl.

108
00:07:05,575 --> 00:07:09,411
All pods are running: two backend, two frontend, and Postgres.

109
00:07:09,411 --> 00:07:13,680
Then the services, the ingress on careslot.local, and the autoscaler.

110
00:07:13,680 --> 00:07:17,455
Helm list shows the staging release and the monitoring stack.

111
00:07:17,455 --> 00:07:21,662
Both deployments run the image tagged with the commit I just pushed.

112
00:07:21,662 --> 00:07:24,137
And the API answers through the ingress.

113
00:07:27,533 --> 00:07:29,511
Now the autoscaler under load.

114
00:07:29,511 --> 00:07:33,268
I start a load test with sixty workers and watch the HPA.

115
00:07:33,268 --> 00:07:39,332
CPU jumps well past the sixty percent target, and replicas climb from two, to three, to six,

116
00:07:39,332 --> 00:07:40,123
the maximum.

117
00:07:40,123 --> 00:07:41,507
This clip is sped up.

118
00:07:42,400 --> 00:07:46,777
In Prometheus, the targets page shows the CareSlot backend service monitor,

119
00:07:46,777 --> 00:07:50,219
with every backend pod up, six out of six while scaled out.

120
00:07:50,219 --> 00:07:55,355
A query for requests per second per pod shows the traffic spreading across the new pods.

121
00:07:55,355 --> 00:08:00,081
And the custom business metric counts appointments booked, labelled by specialty,

122
00:08:00,081 --> 00:08:01,890
coming from the app's own code.

123
00:08:04,300 --> 00:08:07,059
Finally, Grafana, live during the load test.

124
00:08:07,059 --> 00:08:09,631
Requests per second climb past a hundred.

125
00:08:09,631 --> 00:08:13,080
Latency percentiles, the 5xx error ratio stays at zero,

126
00:08:13,080 --> 00:08:17,846
and the backend pod count goes from three to six as the autoscaler responds.

127
00:08:17,846 --> 00:08:20,418
CPU per pod spreads out as new pods join.

128
00:08:20,418 --> 00:08:25,999
Further down are bookings by specialty, and status changes with rejected double-bookings.

129
00:08:25,999 --> 00:08:29,699
The dashboard is shipped as a config map in the Helm chart,

130
00:08:29,699 --> 00:08:34,842
so Grafana loads it automatically, and it's versioned in Git like everything else.

131
00:08:34,842 --> 00:08:36,473
This clip is also sped up.

